Let the host set the arm64 thread pointer in the ANDRUNA build.

Andruna runs bionic as Android ELF inside a Windows process (ADR 0013).
On Windows ARM64, TPIDR_EL0 is documented as reserved, and whether a user
write to it survives context switches is not established (spike S4).
Under ANDRUNA, __set_tls calls the host function __andruna_set_tls, which
sets the thread pointer for the current thread by whatever mechanism the
platform needs and registers it. Reading it (__get_tls) is unchanged.

diff --git a/libc/platform/bionic/tls.h b/libc/platform/bionic/tls.h
--- a/libc/platform/bionic/tls.h
+++ b/libc/platform/bionic/tls.h
@@ -38,9 +38,21 @@
   return result;
 }
 
+#if defined(ANDRUNA)
+// The host owns the thread pointer register (TPIDR_EL0 is reserved on
+// Windows); it sets it and keeps it for this thread.
+__BEGIN_DECLS
+void __andruna_set_tls(void* tls);
+__END_DECLS
+
+static inline void __set_tls(void* tls) {
+  __andruna_set_tls(tls);
+}
+#else
 static inline void __set_tls(void* tls) {
   __asm__("msr tpidr_el0, %0" : : "r" (tls));
 }
+#endif
 
 #elif defined(__arm__)
 
